<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Elisabeth of the Palatinate</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1618–1680</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Princess Elisabeth of Bohemia</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Elisabeth of the Palatinate was a German princess and philosopher, the eldest daughter of Frederick V, the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Winter King</a>' of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. Raised in exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, she carried on a celebrated correspondence with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-descartes">René Descartes</a> from 1643 on the relation of mind and body, and he dedicated his Principles of Philosophy to her. From 1667 until her death she was abbess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herford">Herford</a>, where she sheltered religious dissenters.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/elisabeth-of-the-palatinate.jpg" alt="Elisabeth of the Palatinate" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gerard van Honthorst · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1636_Elisabeth_of_Bohemia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
